The detection function concerns only r = d/sigma, as explained. As I think about it: "non-euclidean" distances mess with the definition of d, putting it on an arbitrary new scale, and fixing sigma to a constant ensures that r varies in proportion to the new d (and is controlled by the model for noneuc, not the model for sigma). Whether sigma=1 or anything else is not material, except that it will change the absolute value of coefficients (and required starting values).

